disc(f) = 2951479051793528258560000  =  {2: 72, 5: 4}
Bad primes (divide disc): {2,5}.  All other p are GOOD reduction.
  p=7: disc mod p = 2  -> good reduction: True; p>2g=6: True
  p=19: disc mod p = 17  -> good reduction: True; p>2g=6: True
  p=3: disc mod p = 1  -> good reduction: True; p>2g=6: False

Coleman 1985 unconditional bound: for p good, p>2g,
  |C(Q)| <= #C(F_p) + 2g - 2.
This form does NOT require checking residue-disk differential non-vanishing
(that refinement, needed for the sharp #residue-disk count, is Magma/Coleman-
integration territory).  The +2g-2 bound is unconditional given rank J < g.

At p=7 (good, 7>6): #C(F_7)=8 => |C'(Q)| <= 12.  Known points = 8.
